<item>
  <title>What randomization actually buys you (and what it doesn’t)</title>
  <link>https://entejar.github.io/posts/what-randomization-buys-you/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<div class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR</strong> — Randomization doesn't remove noise, it removes <em>bias</em>. It makes the treatment independent of the potential outcomes, which is what licenses the difference-in-means as an unbiased effect estimate. Variance is a separate problem — and that's where tricks like CUPED come in.</div>
<p>There’s a common misreading of what an A/B test gives you: the sense that because you randomized, the number you read off is <em>the truth</em>. Randomization is powerful, but it solves exactly one problem — and it’s worth being precise about which one, because the other problems don’t go away.</p>
<section id="the-setup-two-outcomes-one-of-which-you-never-see" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-setup-two-outcomes-one-of-which-you-never-see">The setup: two outcomes, one of which you never see</h2>
<p>For each unit <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?i">, imagine two potential outcomes: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y_i(1)"> if treated and <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y_i(0)"> if not. The individual treatment effect is <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctau_i%20=%20Y_i(1)%20-%20Y_i(0)">. The catch — the <em>fundamental problem of causal inference</em> — is that you only ever observe one of them:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y_i%20=%20T_i%20%5C,%20Y_i(1)%20+%20(1%20-%20T_i)%5C,%20Y_i(0)."></p>
<p>So we lower our sights from <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctau_i"> to the <strong>average treatment effect</strong>, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctext%7BATE%7D%20=%20%5Cmathbb%7BE%7D%5BY_i(1)%20-%20Y_i(0)%5D">.</p>
</section>
<section id="what-randomization-does" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-randomization-does">What randomization does</h2>
<p>The natural estimator is the difference in group means. It’s only trustworthy if the treated and control groups would have looked the same <em>absent treatment</em>. Randomization guarantees that by making treatment <strong>independent of the potential outcomes</strong>:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5C%7BY_i(0),%20Y_i(1)%5C%7D%20%5Cperp%5C!%5C!%5C!%5Cperp%20T_i."></p>
<p>Under that independence the difference in means is unbiased for the ATE. That’s the whole trick. It’s a statement about <strong>bias</strong>, not noise.</p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p>Randomization buys you exchangeability. It does not buy you precision.</p>
</blockquote>
</section>
<section id="what-it-doesnt-do" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="what-it-doesnt-do">What it doesn’t do</h2>
<p>Your estimate still has variance, and with a skewed metric — revenue, watch-time, anything zero-inflated — that variance can be large enough to hide a real effect. The cheapest fix is <strong>CUPED</strong>: use a pre-experiment covariate <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> correlated with <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y"> but, by construction, unaffected by treatment, and adjust</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?Y%5E%7B%5Ctext%7Bcuped%7D%7D%20=%20Y%20-%20%5Ctheta%5C,(X%20-%20%5Cbar%20X),%20%5Cqquad%20%5Ctheta%20=%20%5Cfrac%7B%5Coperatorname%7BCov%7D(Y,%20X)%7D%7B%5Coperatorname%7BVar%7D(X)%7D."></p>
<p>Because <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> is independent of treatment, this leaves the estimate unbiased but shrinks its variance by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?1%20-%20%5Crho%5E2">, where <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Crho%20=%20%5Coperatorname%7Bcorr%7D(Y,%20X)">. One subtlety worth stating, because it’s easy to get wrong: use a <em>single</em> <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Ctheta"> and center <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?X"> by the <strong>pooled</strong> mean across both arms. Center each arm by its own mean and the correction cancels out — you get your noisy estimate right back. Let’s see it work:</p>
