<p>A pre-period metric correlated at <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Crho%20=%200.6"> cuts variance by about a third — for free, with no extra users and no change to the estimand.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-takeaway" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-takeaway">The takeaway</h2>
<p>Keep the two jobs separate. <strong>Identification</strong> — getting an unbiased target — is what randomization (or a credible quasi-experimental design) handles. <strong>Estimation</strong> — getting a tight, decision-useful number — is a variance problem you attack with covariate adjustment, stratification, or a better estimator. Conflating them is how teams end up either trusting a noisy readout or blaming randomization for a problem it was never meant to solve.</p>
<p>Next time: what to do when you <em>can’t</em> randomize, and geo-level quasi-experiments become the tool of choice.</p>


